--- body ---
## Context

`spec/README.md` states where each of the twenty-eight requirements stands,
and its `evidence` column says how each verdict was established. Today that
column reads **6 measured, 14 read in the source, 8 inferred**: only six
verdicts rest on something that ran.

This is not a defect in the requirements. Since the RFC 2119 pass they are
enumerable — forty MUST and MUST NOT clauses across the twenty-eight, each a
property a harness could assert. The gap is that the harness does not exist,
so a status page has to be read rather than computed, and a reader has to
trust the reader.

This tracker exists so that gap stays visible while the work is deferred. It
closes when `evidence` reads all-measured, or when the author rules that some
rows will stay read-only and says which.

## The unit of work

The forty MUST clauses, not the twenty-eight requirements. A requirement is
compound — R12 is two clauses, R24 is three, R13 is three — and grading a
compound as one token is exactly what made `partial` ambiguous before the
evidence column split it. One assertion per clause makes `delivered` a count
rather than a judgement.

Two clauses need care rather than a test:

- **R26 carries no MUST at all.** The RFC 2119 pass left it declarative;
  ticket 0269 covers the stale sentences that pass could not reach. Its
  wording is also under revision in ticket 0080, so settle the text before
  writing its assertion.
- **R17's single MUST has a conjunctive payload** (N of M, per stage, with
  the most-recent-covered date, metadata-only counted with its reason). That
  is four assertions behind one MUST, so the clause count is a floor.

## Children, already filed — this tracker adds none

Per the author's deferral, no new child is filed. What exists:

- `tickets/0029` — the golden fixture corpus and a Zotero-free index harness.
  The precondition for nearly every clause: no fixture library, no assertion.
- `tickets/0026` — repo-side gates: fold, golden, RSS, convergence, soak.
  Directly converts R19, R20, R21 and R26 from unwatched to gated, and R19
  and R20 are the two whose own text demands a check that RUNS.
- `tickets/0032` — the harness offer, the acceptance spec handed upstream.
  The same assertions, in a form the maintainer can run against whatever he
  builds; the durable value if he reimplements the machinery in his own idiom.

Neither `Blocked-by` nor blocking: a tracker is not a prerequisite of its
children (tickets/AGENTS.md), and each child already carries its own.

## Where the twenty-two sit

`inferred` (8) — R13, R14, R15, R18, R21, R25, R26, R27. Nothing was opened
or run; the verdicts rest on merged pull requests, design documents and
reasoning. These are the rows to distrust first.

`code` (14) — R1, R2, R3, R4, R8, R9, R10, R11, R16, R17, R22, R23, R24, R28.
The upstream source was read at the reviewed baseline. Better than inferred,
and still not a test: reading that a cap exists is not the same as watching a
monster document get truncated.

## Invariants

- The evidence vocabulary stays closed (`measured`, `code`, `inferred`) and
  `bench/check_progress.py` keeps recomputing the tally. A row may not be
  upgraded to `measured` without the artifact that measured it.
- Upgrading a row is a claim about a release, so it is re-read when the
  reviewed baseline moves — the guard already fails the build on that.
- No design number, threshold or measurement enters `spec/README.md`; the
  artifacts live in `bench/results/` and the thresholds in DESIGN.md.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] every MUST clause of the twenty-eight has an assertion, or an explicit
      ruling that it will not
- [ ] `spec/README.md`'s evidence tally reads all-measured, or names the
      exceptions and why
- [ ] the delivered column is derived from the clause results rather than
      assigned by a reader
- [ ] children 0026, 0029 and 0032 closed, and the integration review of
      tickets/AGENTS.md run over their combined diff
